# What espix runs at boot: one unit per line, in the order given.
#
#   <name> [always | once | every <interval>] [arguments...]
#
# The name is looked up among the shell's commands and its builtin services.
# With no policy the unit is started once at boot; "always" starts it and
# restarts it if it exits; "every 6h" runs it again on that schedule. A unit
# is started by the system rather than by anyone who authenticated, so it runs
# as root -- there is no sudo in this file, and a unit that needs privilege
# simply has it.
#
# This is the example. /etc/units is what the system actually reads, and it is
# created on the first boot; copy this file over it to change what runs.

# --- WireGuard -------------------------------------------------------------
# Bring the VPN server up at boot. Until this line runs, nothing of the VPN
# exists -- no wg0, no peer table, no task, no memory -- so leaving it
# commented costs nothing at all. The clients in /etc/vpn/clients are admitted
# from /etc/vpn/peers as wg0 comes up, so after a reboot this one line is all
# the VPN needs.
#
# vpn up

# --- The shape of the other policies ---------------------------------------
# A builtin service, restarted whenever it exits:
#
#   sshd always
#
# A oneshot on a schedule:
#
#   poll every 6h upgrade --check
